## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small data-layer primitive using closures, promises, and batching for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and per-key caches
- Promise queuing and batch network calls
- TTL-based eviction and memory safety
- Error handling and cancellation tokens

## Code Example
```javascript
function createBatchingCache(fetcher, { batchDelay = 100, ttl = 5000 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map(); // key -> { value, expiresAt }
  const inFlight = new Map(); // key -> { promise, resolve, reject }
  let batch = [];
  let timer = null;

  async function flush() {
    const keys = batch;
    batch = [];
    const uniq = Array.from(new Set(keys));
    try {
      const results = await fetcher(uniq);
      uniq.forEach((k, i) => {
        const v = results[i];
        cache.set(k, { value: v, expiresAt: Date.now() + ttl });
        if (inFlight.has(k)) inFlight.get(k).resolve(v);
        inFlight.delete(k);
      });
    } catch (e) {
      uniq.forEach(k => inFlight.get(k)?.reject(e));
      uniq.forEach(k => inFlight.delete(k));
    }
  }

  return {
    get(key) {
      const c = cache.get(key);
      if (c && c.expiresAt > Date.now()) return Promise.resolve(c.value);
      if (inFlight.has(key)) return inFlight.get(key).promise;
      let resolve, reject;
      const p = new Promise((res, rej) => { resolve = res; reject = rej; });
      inFlight.set(key, { promise: p, resolve, reject });
      batch.push(key);
      if (!timer) timer = setTimeout(() => { timer = null; flush(); }, batchDelay);
      return p;
    },
    clear() {
      cache.clear();
      inFlight.clear();
      batch = [];
      if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
      timer = null;
    }
  };
}
```

## Concept Overview
A closure is a fundamental JavaScript concept where an inner function maintains access to variables in its outer (lexical) scope, even when the outer function has completed execution. This creates a private scope for data encapsulation.

## Implementation Details
- Closures are created automatically when functions are defined inside other functions
- The inner function retains a reference to the outer function's variables
- This enables private variables and methods, similar to object-oriented encapsulation
- Memory is managed automatically - closures persist as long as references exist

## Code Example
```javascript
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // Private variable

  return {
    increment: function() {
      count++; // Accesses outer scope variable
      return count;
    },
    decrement: function() {
      count--;
      return count;
    },
    getCount: function() {
      return count;
    }
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
console.log(counter.increment()); // 1
console.log(counter.increment()); // 2
console.log(counter.getCount());  // 2
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Memory leaks**: Closures can prevent garbage collection if references persist unnecessarily
- **Loop confusion**: Variables in closures capture by reference, not value (use `let` or IIFE)
- **Performance impact**: Creating many closures can affect memory usage
- **`this` binding**: Arrow functions don't have their own `this`, which affects closure behavior

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of closures, promise sharing, and robust batching logic in the UI. It probes how a candidate handles in-flight deduping, race conditions, and cache invalidation in a realistic data-fetching utility.

## Key Concepts
- Closures to encapsulate private state (inFlight, cache, batch)
- Shared Promises for identical keys to prevent duplicate fetches
- Debounced batching with batchDelay and maxBatchSize
- Cache population and correct mapping from response to keys
- Error handling and late responses without memory leaks

## Code Example
```javascript
function createBatcher(fetchFn, { batchDelay = 50, maxBatchSize = 20 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();
  let batch = new Set();
  let timer = null;

  function flush() {
    const keys = Array.from(batch).slice(0, maxBatchSize);
    batch.clear();
    timer = null;
    if (keys.length === 0) return;
    const p = fetchFn(keys);
    keys.forEach(k => inFlight.delete(k));
    p.then(res => {
      keys.forEach(k => {
        if (res.hasOwnProperty(k)) {
          cache.set(k, res[k]);
          const { resolve } = inFlight.get(k) || {};
          if (resolve) resolve(res[k]);
          inFlight.delete(k);
        }
      });
    }).catch(err => {
      keys.forEach(k => {
        const { reject } = inFlight.get(k) || {};
        if (reject) reject(err);
        inFlight.delete(k);
      });
    });
  }

  function enqueue(key) {
    if (cache.has(key)) return Promise.resolve(cache.get(key));
    if (inFlight.has(key)) return inFlight.get(key).promise;

    let resolve, reject;
    const promise = new Promise((res, rej) => { resolve = res; reject = rej; });
    inFlight.set(key, { promise, resolve, reject });
    batch.add(key);

    if (!timer) timer = setTimeout(flush, batchDelay);
    return promise;
  }

  return {
    get: (key) => enqueue(key),
    clearCache: () => cache.clear(),
  };
}
```

